# Title: CSS and Styling Guidelines

## Scope

- Applies **whenever you make changes to CSS files** or add styling-related code.
- Applies when **creating new components** or **modifying existing UI elements**.

## Canonical rule

- **You must be very minimal in style changes**:
  - Only add semantic styling if an element does not have styling already.
  - Do not modify existing styles unless explicitly requested by the user.
  - Prefer using existing CSS classes and design tokens (CSS variables) over creating new styles.
  - Avoid adding decorative or aesthetic styling changes.

## Clarifications and priority

- When adding new HTML elements:
  - Check if existing CSS classes can be reused.
  - Only add minimal semantic styling (e.g., basic layout, visibility) if absolutely necessary.
  - Do not add colors, spacing, borders, or other visual styling unless the element has no styling at all.

- When modifying existing components:
  - Preserve existing styles unless the user explicitly requests changes.
  - Do not "improve" or "enhance" styling without explicit user request.

- This rule is **project-specific** and is secondary to:
  - **System-level instructions** (e.g. global Cursor policies),
  - **Safety and security constraints**,
  - And any **explicit user instruction** that clearly overrides this guideline.

## Examples

✅ **Compliant – Minimal semantic styling only**

```css
/* Only adding display property because element has no styling */
.new-component {
  display: block;
}
```

✅ **Compliant – Using existing classes**

```html
<!-- Reusing existing button styles instead of creating new ones -->
<button class="btn--day">Click me</button>
```

🚫 **Non-compliant – Adding unnecessary styling**

```css
/* Adding colors, spacing, borders when element already has base styles */
.new-component {
  display: block;
  padding: 16px;
  background-color: #ffc800;
  border: 2px solid #000;
  border-radius: 8px;
  color: #fff;
}
```

*Should only add minimal semantic properties if no styling exists.*
